## Scientific design to implement

Integrate the accepted preferred answers surgically and coherently. In particular, the revised B1 must make the following design assessable.

### 1. Hyperspectral-to-Sentinel-2 transfer

Specify a restricted, testable common-predictor chain:

* M1 archive audit covering instrument metadata, wavelength coverage, calibration records, measurement footprint, plot geometry, canopy conditions, treatments, dates and matched physiological observations.
* Synthetic Sentinel-2 band calculation through integration of calibrated reflectance with versioned Sentinel-2 spectral-response functions.
* Calculation of indices after band integration.
* Exclusion of bands that cannot be reconstructed from the available spectral range; no invention of SWIR information from VNIR-only data.
* Separation of proximal white/dark-reference correction from Sentinel-2 atmospheric processing.
* Use of Sentinel-2 Level-2A surface reflectance with cloud/shadow quality filtering.
* A common 20 m analysis grid when 20 m bands are used.
* Explicit interior-pixel and crop-purity rules.
* The accepted engineering screen: median absolute index discrepancy no greater than 0.25 historical standard deviations and no more than 10% change in stress classifications on the locked sensitivity set.
* Paired prospective canopy spectra on selected campaigns, aligned with actual Sentinel-2 observations and physiology, to diagnose sensing discrepancies.
* Prospective measurements must not tune the frozen primary model or its primary mapping.
* If historical spectral conversion is not defensible, use only the defensible common predictors, retain a soil-water/meteorological operational baseline and report the failed spectral transfer honestly.
* If paired prospective spectra cannot separate sensing and environmental effects, report the combined transfer gap and narrow the associated claim.

Do not claim that the exact archive instrument, wavelength range, field polygons, canopy cover or equipment allocation is known unless the reviewed sources prove it.

### 2. Prospective field experiment

Adopt the preferred proposed planning design as the FIELDWISE protocol target:

* three irrigation regimes;
* four spatial blocks;
* 12 separately managed treatment areas per season;
* randomised treatments within blocks;
* candidate treatment areas of approximately 80 × 80 m;
* a 20 m inward buffer, yielding a nominal 40 × 40 m interior core;
* a nominal treatment footprint of approximately 7.68 ha before access and isolation areas;
* target of 12 physiological campaigns per season;
* five spatially distributed plants per treatment area per campaign;
* up to 288 area-date records before missingness;
* approximately 1,440 plant visits over two seasons;
* an attrition sensitivity scenario of approximately 202 area-date pairs after 30% missingness.

Make clear that plants, pixels and repeated dates are not independent field replicates. The independently managed treatment area is the experimental unit. Two years on one field provide temporal replication, not two independent environments.

Present these figures as adopted design targets whose final implementation is subject to the field-readiness gate. Do not claim that a particular farmer, field, irrigation layout or 7.68 ha site is already secured.

Include growth-stage stratification covering canopy development, flowering/fruit set, fruit filling and ripening, with relevant temperature/VPD, genotype, time-of-day and competing-stress records.

### 3. Physiological stress reference

Define the planned reference before model fitting:

* primary physiological axis: stomatal conductance relative to stage- and campaign-matched well-watered controls;
* corroborating physiological endpoint: leaf water status or another consistently available independent marker;
* fluorescence as complementary evidence rather than an automatic no-stress label;
* numerical reference limits derived from historical controls and measurement repeatability, not optimised against model accuracy or prospective outcomes;
* ambiguous or confounded area-dates classified as indeterminate and retained in sensitivity reporting;
* irrigation treatments and AquaCrop used as imposed-deficit context and agronomic comparators, not as circular substitutes for the physiological outcome.

If a common two-marker reference cannot be established, use the safe response: one continuous physiological endpoint as the primary reference and other measurements as corroboration.

### 4. Model, validation and uncertainty

Name the planned analytical framework:

* primary interpretable model: regularised logistic regression with a limited pre-specified predictor set;
* nonlinear benchmark: constrained gradient-boosted trees;
* outer leave-one-year-out validation across the five historical seasons;
* all imputation, scaling, feature selection, tuning and calibration fitted only inside training folds;
* year/group-aware inner validation;
* final pipeline selected under a declared historical-data rule, refitted using historical data only and frozen before prospective outcomes;
* group-aware or hierarchical uncertainty treatment keeping all observations from a treatment area together;
* separate season-level and pooled reporting;
* out-of-domain and insufficient-quality observations trigger abstention or a limited-evidence flag.

Adopt the preferred project decision criteria:

* balanced accuracy target of at least 0.70 and demonstrated advantage over a locked agronomic comparator;
* positive Brier skill against a frozen reference model;
* historical alert-threshold selection targeting sensitivity of at least 0.80 and false-positive rate no higher than 0.20;
* warning lead time reported as interval-bounded where observation frequency does not support a point estimate;
* failure of these criteria restricts the model to research-only use and restricts the MVP accordingly;
* primary unchanged-model results are always reported before any separately versioned recalibration.

These are FIELDWISE decision criteria, not universal physiological standards or promised performance results.

